# Notice — what the MIT licence in LICENSE does and does not cover

`LICENSE` (MIT) covers the code written for this project.

## ⚑ Three files are NOT covered, because they are not ours to license

    app/src/generator/vendor/lib/build_docs.js
    app/src/generator/vendor/lib/docx_kit.js
    app/src/generator/vendor/lib/sections.js

These are copied **byte-verbatim** from the ARES CBE generation system
(`markknit/cbe-generation-system`, pinned commit `742c8a9`, vendored 2026-07-19 — see
`app/src/generator/vendor/PROVENANCE.md` for the SHA-256 table). Publishing them in a public
repository redistributes them, and the right to do that has to be granted by their owner. Until that
grant exists in writing, the MIT declaration above should not be read as applying to them.

This is recorded as an open item rather than assumed: a licence file that quietly claims to cover
someone else's code is worse than one that names the gap.

## Lesson-plan content is a separate question

The lesson plans themselves (ARES and SeaVuria) are **content**, not software, and a software licence
is the wrong instrument for them — Creative Commons themselves advise against CC licences for code,
and the reverse holds too. A content licence (CC BY 4.0 is the candidate) is a separate conversation
with ARES and SeaVuria.

⚑ If content does arrive under an attribution licence, note that attribution is the *condition* of
using it, and these deliverables travel by email and USB with no application around them — so nothing
carries the credit unless the generated document itself does. That is a generator change, not a
user-interface one (SPEC §4).

## Dependencies

Third-party npm packages and container images keep their own licences; they are not redistributed by
this repository. Two worth knowing, both verified rather than recalled: Payload CMS is MIT (the reason
MIT was chosen for this project, so there is no friction between them), and Gotenberg — the PDF
service — is MIT.
